## What we saw

Using `@flue/runtime` 1.0.0-beta.2 with the `local()` sandbox: when the model emits several `edit` calls for the same file in one assistant message, the file sometimes ends up wrong afterwards even though every call reported success:

- only one of the batch's edits is actually applied — the others are silently lost;
- the file sometimes gains garbage at the end (fragments of pre-edit text after the final `}`), breaking its syntax;
- occasionally an edit fails with `Could not find the exact text` for text that is definitely in the file.

Edits made one per message never had a problem.

## What we think is happening

The `edit` tool does `readFile → replace → writeFile` with no synchronization, and the agent runs a batch's tool calls concurrently (`toolExecution: "parallel"` into pi-agent-core, which `Promise.all`s the executes). So all edits in a batch read the same snapshot and the last write wins — losing the other edits. The garbage looks like the `fs.writeFile` truncate-at-open behavior: with two writes in flight, a shorter one finishing after a longer one leaves the longer write's tail past the end. Behavior looks the same in beta.9's `createEditTool`.

## Minimal repro

```js
import fs from "node:fs/promises";

const file = "/tmp/repro.txt";
await fs.writeFile(file, "AAAA BBBB CCCC\n".repeat(100));

// same steps as the edit tool
async function edit(oldText, newText) {
  const content = await fs.readFile(file, "utf8");
  if (!content.includes(oldText)) throw new Error("Could not find the exact text");
  await fs.writeFile(file, content.replace(oldText, newText));
}

// two edits in one assistant message
await Promise.all([
  edit("AAAA", "AAAA-MUCH-LONGER-REPLACEMENT"),  // grows the file
  edit("BBBB", "B2"),                             // shrinks it
]);

const out = await fs.readFile(file, "utf8");
console.log("first edit applied:", out.includes("MUCH-LONGER"));   // false
console.log("trailing garbage:", JSON.stringify(out.slice(-40)));  // tail of the longer write
```

## In the wild

Condensed from one session transcript. The model makes 4 edits to the same file in one message; all succeed:

