Flue ships with no identity layer. That's fine for isolated agents, but once agents are calling external services or accepting calls from other agents, there's no built-in way to say "this is agent X" with cryptographic proof.

I built `@agentlair/flue` to handle this. It wraps your handler with `withAgentLair`, which issues an EdDSA JWT at session start. Any service can verify the token offline via JWKS at `agentlair.dev/.well-known/jwks.json`. No shared secrets, no API key on the receiving end.

Quick example:

```typescript
import { withAgentLair, mcpOptions } from '@agentlair/flue';

export default withAgentLair(
  async ({ init, payload, env, aal }) => {
    const partnerTools = await connectMcpServer(
      'partner',
      mcpOptions('https://api.partner.com/mcp', aal),
    );

    const agent = await init({ model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6' });
    const session = await agent.session();
    return await session.prompt(payload.message as string, { tools: [partnerTools] });
  },
  { audience: 'https://api.partner.com', scopes: ['mcp:tools:execute'] },
);
```

`aal.bearer` is a ready-to-use `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header string. Works for direct HTTP calls or MCP connections.

For inbound verification on a Flue webhook:

```typescript
import { verifyIncomingAAT } from '@agentlair/flue';

const caller = await verifyIncomingAAT(payload.headers?.authorization, {
  audience: 'https://my-service.com',
  requiredScopes: ['mcp:tools:execute'],
});
// caller.accountId persists across sessions
// caller.auditUrl links to the full audit trail
```

JWKS only on the receiving side. No API key.

