Workflow args passed via `flue.args` are currently untyped — they come in as a plain object and every skill that reads them has to trust that the caller got it right. There's no schema validation at the boundary, so a missing or misspelled arg silently propagates until something breaks deep inside a skill execution. This also means workflow authors get no autocomplete or type errors when defining their args, and skill authors have to defensively check for every field. Should we consider letting workflows declare an args schema upfront, so that `flue.args` is fully typed and validated before the workflow runs?

```ts
// Example Possible API
import { Flue, z } from '@flue/client';

export default flue.workflow({
  args: {
    issueNumber: z.number(),
    triageDir: z.string().default('triage/current'),
    skipFix: z.boolean().optional(),
  },
  async run(flue) {
    // flue.args is typed: { issueNumber: number; triageDir: string; skipFix?: boolean }
    const { issueNumber, triageDir } = flue.args;
    await flue.skill('triage/reproduce.md', { triageDir });
  },
});
```

